    ],
    whatTitle: "What is an animation clip?",
    what: [
      `An animation clip (clip) is a file that says <strong>which property of which object to set to what value, and when</strong>. Its extension is <code>.anim</code>. You use it by putting it in an Animator state. ${tipLink("animator-basics", "controller")} explains how states and clips relate.`,
      "The Animator writes the values in the current state's clip to the avatar <strong>every frame</strong>. As long as it stays in that state, it keeps writing the same values.",
      "For example, the clip <code>Hat_ON</code>, which shows the hat, contains just this one row.",
    ],
    clipTableLabel: "Contents of the Hat_ON clip (example)",
    clipHead: ["Path (which object)", "Type", "Property (what)", "Frame 0", "Frame 1"],
    clipRows: [
      ["Accessories/Hat", "GameObject", "Is Active", "1 (on)", "1 (on)"],
    ],
    what2: [
      "Each row (property) is made of a <strong>path</strong> (which object in the avatar) and a <strong>property</strong> (what to change on that object). A mark in the row for the value at a certain time is called a <strong>keyframe</strong> (key).",
      "In the Animation window, this row appears as <code>Hat : Is Active</code>: the last name in the path, then the property name.",
      `For an on/off gimmick, make both a clip that shows the hat (Hat_ON) and a clip that hides it (Hat_OFF). There are ways to skip the off clip, but then how it behaves depends on the Write Defaults setting. If you make both, it works the same whether Write Defaults is ON or OFF (${tipLink("unity-vs-vrchat", "write-defaults")}).`,
    ],
    canTitle: "What to move from FX, and what not to",
    can: [
      `Most gimmick clips go into the FX controller. From FX, you can move almost anything except Humanoid bones (muscles). If you move BlendShapes or materials from a slot other than FX, they aren't copied to the avatar in the mirror (${tipLink("animator-basics", "layers")}).`,
